Third-party compression libraries linked into the port — NOT part of
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

To fill format gaps in upstream 26.03 (no zstd encoder, no lz4, no brotli, no
lzip, no snappy), the port links four third-party libraries statically into the
application binary. They are not vendored in this repository; the build
obtains them from Homebrew (or any equivalent build) and degrades gracefully
when they are absent.

    Zstandard (libzstd.a)     Meta Platforms, Inc. and contributors
                              BSD 3-clause  https://github.com/facebook/zstd
    LZ4 (liblz4.a)            Yann Collet    BSD 2-clause
                              https://github.com/lz4/lz4
    Brotli (libbrotli*.a)     Google LLC     MIT
                              https://github.com/google/brotli
    liblzma / XZ Utils        Lasse Collin and contributors
    (liblzma.a)               0BSD (liblzma) https://github.com/tukaani-project/xz

All four are permissive licenses that allow binary redistribution provided the
copyright notice and license text are retained. The full texts therefore travel
with every distribution channel, under third-party/ next to this file's copies
(7-Zip.app/Contents/Resources/licenses/third-party/, and the doc directories of
the pkg/dmg and the Homebrew tarball). Per-component detail, including the
xxHash code embedded in libzstd, is in THIRD_PARTY.md, section 1.3.

Note: Snappy support is a first-party implementation in dist/engine/Z7ExtCodec.cpp
(CSnappyDecoder / CSnappyEncoder), released under this port's own license
(LGPL-2.1-or-later). No third-party snappy code is compiled in; only the public
format specification from https://github.com/google/snappy (BSD 3-clause) was
followed.


